No — raise the pool size and you'll just leak faster. The real bug is in `db.js:11-16`:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  const result = await fn(client);
  client.release();
  return result;
}
```

If `fn(client)` throws, `client.release()` is never called — the connection is checked out forever. That matches pool.log exactly: `idle=0` never recovers and `waiting` climbs monotonically (7→11→14→19→23→28) instead of fluctuating with load, which is the signature of a leak, not genuine demand exceeding capacity.

Fix the leak first:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Once that's in, watch pool.log again — if `waiting` still saturates under real traffic, then bumping `max` is reasonable. Right now it would just buy time before hitting the new ceiling.

